/**
\page parameters Parameter reference

Every argument of every sampler. A default is the value used when the argument is omitted. A
value outside its valid range makes `define(...)` throw `std::invalid_argument`.

\tableofcontents

\section params_shared Conventions shared by all samplers

\subsection params_theta Thermal speeds: theta_perp, theta_par

`theta` is a thermal speed, not a temperature, and has units of velocity. For the
bi-Maxwellian, `theta = sqrt(2 k_B T / m)` and a velocity component is distributed as
`N(0, theta^2 / 2)`. For the bi-Kappa distribution, `theta` is the scale in its density and is
not tied to a temperature, which does not exist for `kappa <= 3/2`. The bi-Kappa distribution is

    f(v) ~ [1 + v_perp^2 / (kappa theta_perp^2) + v_par^2 / (kappa theta_par^2)]^-(kappa+1)

`theta_perp` applies to both directions perpendicular to `ub` and `theta_par` to the direction
along it. Both must be positive.

\subsection params_ub Field direction: ub

A 3-vector `{bx, by, bz}` giving the magnetic-field direction in the global frame.

- It is normalized internally, so it need not have unit length.
- It must be non-zero.
- Samples are drawn in field-aligned coordinates and rotated into the global frame before they
  are returned. For `ub = {0, 0, 1}` no rotation is applied.

The two perpendicular axes are chosen internally. They matter only for a finite velocity cap,
whose box is aligned with them (see \ref params_cap).

\subsection params_seed Seeding and random engines

`seed(s)` can be called at any time. `bi_kappa_distribution` and `bi_maxwellian_distribution`
also accept the seed as the last argument of `define(...)`.

| Value | Effect |
| ----- | ------ |
| `s >= 0` | The same seed reproduces the same sequence. |
| `s < 0`  | Seeds from `std::random_device`. |

Without a seed the internal engine is seeded from `std::random_device` at construction, so runs
are not reproducible.

`dist(gen)` draws from an engine you supply instead of the internal one, for example one
`std::mt19937` shared by several samplers. For `bi_kappa_distribution` the engine must produce a
power-of-two number of distinct values, as `std::mt19937` and `std::mt19937_64` do.

For `bi_kappa_distribution`, a given seed gives the same values under libc++ and libstdc++ on
the same architecture. Compile with `-ffp-contract=off` to rule out differences from fused
multiply-add. Values are not bitwise identical across architectures, and they differ from
release 1.0.0 (see CHANGELOG.md).

\subsection params_rejection Rejection-sampler settings: probe_points, max_reject_tries

These apply to the three samplers that take a user function (`general_velocity_generator`,
`field_aligned_velocity_generator`, `general_position_generator`).

| Parameter | Meaning | Default |
| --------- | ------- | ------- |
| `probe_points` | Number of grid points used once, in `define(...)`, to estimate the maximum of your function. The estimate plus a 5% margin is the rejection envelope. | `1024` (velocity generators, must be `>= 64`); `4096` (position generator, must be `> 0`) |
| `max_reject_tries` | Candidate draws allowed per sample before `operator()` throws `std::runtime_error`. | `100000` (velocity generators); `200000` (position generator) |

\warning If your function has a peak narrower than the probe spacing, the estimated maximum is
too low and the samples are biased, with no error reported. For a narrow peak, raise
`probe_points` until `pdfUpperBound()` stops increasing.

If a sampler exhausts `max_reject_tries`, the function is usually small over most of its range.
Narrowing the range is more effective than raising the limit.

\section params_bikappa bi_kappa_distribution

    define(kappa, theta_perp, theta_par, ub, max_normalized_velocity, seed)

| Parameter | Meaning | Default | Valid range |
| --------- | ------- | ------- | ----------- |
| `kappa` | Spectral index. Smaller values give heavier suprathermal tails; `kappa -> infinity` gives the bi-Maxwellian. | `2.0` | `> 0.5` |
| `theta_perp` | Thermal speed perpendicular to `ub`. | `1.0` | `> 0` |
| `theta_par` | Thermal speed along `ub`. | `1.0` | `> 0` |
| `ub` | Field direction. See \ref params_ub. | `{0, 0, 1}` | non-zero |
| `max_normalized_velocity` | Velocity cap in thermal speeds, or `no_cap()`. See \ref params_cap. | `20` | `> 0`, or `no_cap()` |
| `seed` | See \ref params_seed. | from `std::random_device` | any `int` |

`define(...)` also rejects NaN values, infinite thermal speeds and non-finite `ub` components.

Moments of order `l` exist only for `kappa > (l + 1) / 2`. For `0.5 < kappa <= 1.5` the
uncapped distribution has no finite second moment, so a temperature computed from the loaded
particles does not converge as the number of particles grows. Use quantiles to characterize such
a sample.

\subsection params_precision Finite precision near kappa = 1/2

As `kappa` approaches `1/2`, the bi-Kappa distribution puts a non-negligible probability on
speeds above the largest representable floating-point number. For
`theta_perp = theta_par = 1` and `ub = {0, 0, 1}`, this probability is about `8.3e-4` at `kappa = 0.505` and `6.8e-7` at
`kappa = 0.51` in `double`, and about `1.4e-4` at `kappa = 0.55` and `1.9e-8` at `kappa = 0.60`
in `float`.

- Without a cap, such a draw is returned with infinite components.
- With a cap, it is rejected and redrawn.
- In both cases `n_nonfinite()` counts it, and `n_attempts()` counts every attempt, including
  draws rejected by the cap. `reset()`, `seed()` and `define()` clear both counters.
- An overflowing draw sets `FE_OVERFLOW` and `errno = ERANGE`.

Compiling with `-ffast-math` lets the compiler assume that no infinity occurs, so `no_cap()`
and the counters are unreliable in that mode. The header then prints a compile-time note;
define `BI_KAPPA_ALLOW_FAST_MATH` to silence it. A finite cap still works.

The paper's section on finite precision gives the derivation.

\section params_cap The velocity cap

`max_normalized_velocity` (here `cap`) is a finite positive value or `no_cap()`. It works the
same way on `bi_kappa_distribution` and `bi_maxwellian_distribution`.

With a finite cap, a draw is accepted only if every component lies inside the box

    |v_perp1| / theta_perp <= cap,  |v_perp2| / theta_perp <= cap,  |v_par| / theta_par <= cap

in field-aligned coordinates. Otherwise it is redrawn. The samples therefore follow the
distribution conditioned on the box: the density restricted to the box and renormalized. The
sampler throws `std::runtime_error` after 10^6 consecutive rejections.

- **Default.** The default cap is 20 thermal speeds. Release 1.0.0 used the same default, so
  code written against 1.0.0 samples the same distribution.
- **No cap.** `no_cap()` returns `+infinity`. With it, the sampler draws from the bi-Kappa or
  bi-Maxwellian distribution itself, with no rejection loop. `param().capped()` returns
  `true` when a finite cap is in force. Passing `0` throws.
- **Effect on bi-Kappa.** For `kappa <= 3/2` a cap of 20 removes an appreciable part of the
  high-energy tail. The paper's section on finite velocity bounds quantifies this. The fraction
  of rejected draws is not a reliable measure of how much of the tail is lost.
- **Effect on bi-Maxwellian.** A cap of 20 corresponds to about 28 standard deviations per
  component and has no practical effect.
- **Frame.** The box is aligned with the field-aligned axes and rotates with `ub`. The returned
  global-frame components are therefore not individually bounded by `cap * theta` unless
  `ub = {0, 0, 1}`. The speed is bounded in every frame by
  `cap * sqrt(2 theta_perp^2 + theta_par^2)`.
- **Symmetry.** Because the box is a cube in normalized coordinates, the capped distribution is
  not symmetric under rotation about `ub`.

\code{.cpp}
using BK = bi_kappa_distribution<double>;
BK dist;
dist.define(2.0, 1.0, 2.0, {0, 0, 1});                // cap = 20
dist.define(2.0, 1.0, 2.0, {0, 0, 1}, BK::no_cap());  // no cap
dist.max_normalized_velocity(50.0);                   // change only the cap
bool c = dist.param().capped();                       // true
\endcode

\section params_bimaxwellian bi_maxwellian_distribution

    define(theta_perp, theta_par, ub, max_normalized_velocity, seed)

| Parameter | Meaning | Default | Valid range |
| --------- | ------- | ------- | ----------- |
| `theta_perp` | Thermal speed perpendicular to `ub`. See \ref params_theta. | *required* | `> 0` |
| `theta_par` | Thermal speed along `ub`. | `1.0` | `> 0` |
| `ub` | Field direction. See \ref params_ub. | `{0, 0, 1}` | non-zero |
| `max_normalized_velocity` | Velocity cap in thermal speeds, or `no_cap()`. See \ref params_cap. | `20` | `> 0`, or `no_cap()` |
| `seed` | See \ref params_seed. | from `std::random_device` | any `int` |

\section params_generalvelocity general_velocity_generator

    define(speed_sq_pdf, speed_sq_min, speed_sq_max, probe_points, max_reject_tries)

| Parameter | Meaning | Default | Valid range |
| --------- | ------- | ------- | ----------- |
| `speed_sq_pdf` | Your density `g(w)` in the speed squared `w = \|v\|^2`. It need not be normalized. | *required* | callable; finite, non-negative, positive somewhere in range |
| `speed_sq_min` | Lower end of the sampled `w`. | *required* | `>= 0` |
| `speed_sq_max` | Upper end of the sampled `w`. | *required* | `>= speed_sq_min` |
| `probe_points` | See \ref params_rejection. | `1024` | `>= 64` |
| `max_reject_tries` | See \ref params_rejection. | `100000` | `> 0` |

The speed is `sqrt(w)` and the direction is isotropic. The generator takes no mass: for a
distribution written in energy, use `w = 2E/m`. For example, a Maxwellian with thermal speed
`theta` is `g(w) = sqrt(w) exp(-w / theta^2)`, since `dw = 2 |v| d|v|` and the speed density is
`|v|^2 exp(-|v|^2 / theta^2)`.

`[speed_sq_min, speed_sq_max]` is the support: no sample lies outside it. If
`speed_sq_min == speed_sq_max`, every sample has speed `sqrt(speed_sq_min)` and `g` is not
evaluated.

This interface replaced the 1.0.0 interface `define(f, E_min, E_max, particle_mass, ...)`. An old
four-argument call passes the mass as `probe_points`, and throws if the mass is below 64.

\section params_fieldaligned field_aligned_velocity_generator

    define(speed_sq_pdf, speed_sq_min, speed_sq_max, theta_perp, ub, parallel_sign,
           probe_points, max_reject_tries, max_normalized_velocity)

| Parameter | Meaning | Default | Valid range |
| --------- | ------- | ------- | ----------- |
| `speed_sq_pdf` | Your density `g(w)` for the parallel component, with `w = v_par^2`. | *required* | as above |
| `speed_sq_min` | Lower end of the sampled `v_par^2`. | *required* | `>= 0` |
| `speed_sq_max` | Upper end of the sampled `v_par^2`. | *required* | `>= speed_sq_min` |
| `theta_perp` | Thermal speed of the two Maxwellian perpendicular components, each `N(0, theta_perp^2 / 2)`. | *required* | `> 0` |
| `ub` | Field direction. See \ref params_ub. | *required* | non-zero |
| `parallel_sign` | `+1` puts the parallel component along `ub`, `-1` opposite to it. | *required* | `+1` or `-1` |
| `probe_points` | See \ref params_rejection. | `1024` | `>= 64` |
| `max_reject_tries` | See \ref params_rejection. | `100000` | `> 0` |
| `max_normalized_velocity` | Cap on each perpendicular component, in units of `theta_perp`. A pair with a component above `cap * theta_perp` is redrawn. | `20` | `> 0`, or `no_cap()` |

Every sample has the same sign of `v_par`, which suits beams and drifting populations. For a
symmetric parallel distribution, draw the sign yourself or use `bi_kappa_distribution` or
`bi_maxwellian_distribution`. If `speed_sq_min == speed_sq_max`, every sample has parallel speed
`sqrt(speed_sq_min)`. Velocities are returned in the global frame.

\section params_generalposition general_position_generator

    define(dimension, lower_bounds, upper_bounds, density_function, probe_points,
           max_reject_tries)

| Parameter | Meaning | Default | Valid range |
| --------- | ------- | ------- | ----------- |
| `dimension` | Number of spatial dimensions. | *required* | `1`, `2` or `3` |
| `lower_bounds` | Lower corner of the sampling box, a `std::vector<Real>`. | *required* | size `dimension` |
| `upper_bounds` | Upper corner. | *required* | size `dimension`, each above its lower bound |
| `density_function` | Your density `rho(x)`, taking a `std::vector<Real>` of length `dimension`. It need not be normalized. | *required* | callable; finite, non-negative, positive somewhere |
| `probe_points` | See \ref params_rejection. | `4096` | `> 0` |
| `max_reject_tries` | See \ref params_rejection. | `200000` | `> 0` |

The returned point is a `std::vector<Real>` of length `dimension`.

\section params_errors Exceptions

| Exception | Thrown by | Cause |
| --------- | --------- | ----- |
| `std::invalid_argument` | `define(...)` and the single-parameter setters | A value outside the ranges above, a null callable, or mismatched bound sizes. |
| `std::invalid_argument` | `bi_maxwellian_distribution::operator()` | A zero `ub`. This sampler checks `ub` when it rotates a sample, not in `define(...)`. |
| `std::runtime_error` | `operator()` | A general or field-aligned generator called before `define(...)`; `max_reject_tries` exhausted; 10^6 consecutive cap rejections; or your function returned a negative or non-finite value. |
*/
